// ── D1 round 14: THE FOURTH CHANNEL ───────────────────────────────────────
// `collect_ref_sources_paths` (§B6) is the borrow checker's FOURTH provenance
// walker. It is not a peer of the three the coverage table had columns for
// (`ref_source_places`, `ref_sources_of`, the summarizer's `taint_of`): it
// answers a different question for a DIFFERENT VERDICT — `ref_borrow_sources_`
// is read by `pop_scope` to raise E0597 ("does not live long enough"). So an
// omission here never moved the `c.bump()` verdict that every round from 1 to
// 13 used as its witness, and twelve rounds of shapes could not see it.
//
// Q8 — THE ARM ROUND 13 DELIBERATELY LEFT BEHIND, now with its probe pair.
// P1 widened TupleIndex and IndexRead to `type_may_carry_borrow` and left
// FieldRead on the old `is_borrow_carrying_type` gate with a standing note:
// "if it is widened later it needs its own probe pair." THE PAIR, one variable
// (the aggregate KIND) and nothing else:
//     o = T { a: &x }.a;   admitted rc=0   <- this file
//     o = (&x, 0).0;       refused  rc=1   <- bc_d1r14_q8_tuplelit_twin
// A LITERAL receiver is what makes the witness: `h.r` on a plain variable is
// covered by the RefGraph channel, which is why the shape hid for 13 rounds.
package bc_d1r14_q8_structlit_field;
struct T { a: &i64 }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut o: &i64;
    { let x: i64 = 7i64; o = T { a: &x }.a; }
    return *o as i32;
}
